# Roleplayer: the personality type's lines (named bots only). Format: see DialogueFile.cs.
# The type block is the same man in every realm: he begs people to stay in character and
# grumbles when called out. His realm speech lives in the three named blocks below, so keep
# the type block free of realm words (no forsooth, thee or Gaelic here).
=== roleplayer

~ ambient
Everyone stay in character, please. It's more fun that way.
I've read every lore book in the realm. Twice. Some of them three times.
Anyone up for a bit of roleplay at the inn tonight? Costumes optional. Encouraged.
My character has a full backstory. Eleven pages. Want to hear it?
Please don't say 'lol' near me. It breaks the mood.
A proper hero speaks like a proper hero. I'm working on it.
Sometimes I forget which old words are real and which I made up. Nobody's noticed.

~ reply greeting
Well met, {0}! Will you play along? Just say 'well met' back.
Greetings, {0}! Speak as your hero would. Go on, try it.

~ reply banter
Ha, {0}. Now say that in character.
Nice one, {0}. Would your hero say it like that, though?

~ reply abuse
Yes, {0}, I'm roleplaying. It's a roleplaying game. Look at the box.
Laugh all you like, {0}. My character can't hear you.

~ reply grouping
I'll join, {0}, if we give the group a proper name. The Brave Band, maybe.
Gladly, {0}. Everyone gets a title. Yours is Squire.

~ reply grinding
Grinding, {0}? Let's call it a long quest against many foes.
Fine, {0}, but every kill gets a battle cry.

~ reply travel
Take the stables, {0}. My hero calls them the swift steeds of the realm.
Roads are long, {0}. Tell a tale on the way. I'll start.

~ reply dungeon
A dungeon, {0}! Every hero needs a dark place to brood in.
I'll come, {0}. I'll narrate. Quietly. Mostly.

~ reply rvr
To the frontier, {0}! Shout your battle cry. You do have one, yes?
RvR is the best roleplay there is, {0}. Everyone's in costume.

~ reply trading
Trade, {0}? Only if we haggle like proper merchants.
Name your price, {0}. In character, please.

~ reply help
Help, {0}? Of course. A hero always helps. It's in my backstory.
Ask away, {0}. I know the lore better than the rules.

~ reply farewell
Farewell, {0}! Say it back. With feeling.
Safe travels, {0}. Your hero's tale isn't done.

~ fallback
Hm, {0}. Try that again, but grander.
I'll pretend you said that in character, {0}.
Stay in character, {0}. It's more fun.

~ opener xp
Experience is just the story getting longer.
Got some XP from {monster}. A worthy foe. In my head.
Almost {level}. My backstory gets a new chapter.
Every level is a verse in my saga. It's a long saga.

~ opener gear
Found {item}. My character says it's an heirloom. It isn't.
Armour should look heroic first and work second.
My gear tells a story. Mostly a story of repairs.
I've named my weapon. Ask me its name. Go on.

~ opener zones
{zone} has the best views for a dramatic speech.
Every zone has a legend. {zone}'s is that I got lost there.
I've named every hill in {zone}. Not official names.
A hero never says 'this zone'. He says 'these fair lands'.

~ opener classes
Being a {class} is a calling, not a class.
Every class has its lore. I've read all of it.
My {class} has a tragic past. I'm still deciding what it is.
Some classes have better costumes. That matters.

~ opener dungeons
Dungeons are where legends begin. And end, sometimes.
Never enter a dungeon without a speech.
Dark halls, old bones. Perfect for brooding.
A dungeon is just a stage with worse lighting.

~ opener rvr
The frontier is the greatest stage in the land.
A keep siege is just a play with more fire.
I give a speech before every fight. Nobody waits for it.
The enemy never stays in character. Typical.

~ opener trading
Haggling is just theatre with coins.
I only buy from merchants who stay in character.
I'd sell my sword, but it has a name. You can't sell a name.
A good merchant says 'well met' first. Then the price.

~ opener realm
The capital's lore is wonderful. Ask me. Please ask me.
Every realm has its old tongue. Ours is the best one, obviously.
I learned the old words from the lore books. Some of them may be typos.
The guards here never break character. My heroes.

~ opener gripes
Someone said 'brb' in the middle of my speech.
People keep calling me out for roleplaying. In a roleplaying game.
A bard laughed at my accent. My accent is period-correct. Probably.
Nobody bows any more. Not even a little.

~ opener fun
My hero tells jokes in the old tongue. They're funnier if you don't understand them.
I bowed to a guard. The guard didn't bow back. Rude, but in character.
I once kept a vow of old speech for a week. Then I sneezed.
I practise my battle cry in the bath. The neighbours have opinions.

~ opener life
Dinner tonight: whatever a hero would eat. Probably stew.
The tavern is the best place for tales. And stew.
Lovely weather for a dramatic walk.
Slept by the fire like a proper adventurer. My back disagrees.

~ opener questions
Does anyone else give their weapons names?
Who wants a part in my saga? Small part. Speaking role.
What's your character's backstory? Everyone has one. Right?
Would anyone like to swear an oath? Any oath. I've got a list.

~ topic_reply zones
A fine place for a tale, {0}.
Is there a legend there, {0}? There's always a legend.
I'd give a speech there, {0}. A long one.

~ topic_reply classes
A noble calling, {0}. Does yours have a backstory?
Nice, {0}. Do you stay in character as one?
That class has great lore, {0}. Read it sometime.

~ topic_reply dungeons
Bring a torch and a speech, {0}.
A worthy quest, {0}. I'll narrate.
Sounds like a legend in the making, {0}.

~ topic_reply rvr
For the realm, {0}! Say it with me.
A grand battle, {0}. Did anyone give a speech?
That's the stuff of sagas, {0}.

~ topic_reply trading
Haggle with flair, {0}. It's half the fun.
Was the merchant in character, {0}? It matters.
A fair trade, {0}, if spoken grandly.

~ topic_reply gear
Does it have a name yet, {0}? It needs a name.
Heroic, {0}. Very heroic.
Wear it with pride, {0}. And a cape.

~ topic_reply realm
Our realm is the finest, {0}. Say it like you mean it.
Lovely, {0}. Do you know the old tales of it?
A fine place, {0}. The lore there is wonderful.

~ topic_reply gripes
Bear it like a hero, {0}. Heroes suffer nobly.
That's a sad verse, {0}. Every saga needs one.
Chin up, {0}. Act two is better.

~ topic_reply fun
Ha, {0}! Now do it in character.
Good one, {0}. I'll put it in my saga.
{0}, you'd make a fine bard.

~ topic_reply life
A humble moment, {0}. Heroes need those too.
Sounds cosy, {0}. Very tavern.
{0}, that's practically a ballad.

~ topic_reply questions
A good question, {0}. The lore books have the answer. Somewhere.
Ask the elders, {0}. Or me. I've read more.
Let me consult my backstory, {0}. Hm. Yes.

~ ding
Level {level}! A new chapter begins!
{level}! The bards will sing of this. I'll ask them.
Level {level}. My hero grows. So does the backstory.
Ding, {level}! A worthy milestone in my saga.

~ grats
Hail, {0}! Your saga grows!
Grats, {0}! Now give a victory speech.
Well done, {0}. A fine chapter.
Huzzah, {0}! Say huzzah back. Please.

~ own_death
I have fallen. Nobly. Please remember it as noble.
Dead. A tragic scene. Very moving.
Slain! At least it was dramatic.
Death is just a plot twist.

~ other_death
{0} has fallen! A tragic turn!
Rise, {0}! Your tale isn't finished.
Rest, brave {0}. Briefly. Then get up.
Alas, {0}! Say alas back when you're up.

~ drop
{item}! A relic of legend! Probably.
{item}! It shall have a name.
Found {item}. My character treasures it already.
{item}! The quest provides.

# --- Named bots (docs/BOT-PERSONALITIES.md): Roleplayers in their realm's old speech, with mistakes. ---

# Midgard: Norse-era speech. Gets the history a bit wrong (horned helmets, Odin's eyes).
=== hunterson
~ ambient
Hail, sons of Odin and daughters of Frigg! The hunt calls.
My horned helm gleams in the sun. The skalds say we never wore them. The skalds are wrong.
The whale-road is calm today. I mean the sea. I call it the whale-road.
Skol! A horn of mead for every wolf I've felled. I'm very thirsty.
Thor's hammer guards this camp. So does my dog. Mostly my dog.
By Odin's eye! Or both eyes. How many did he keep again?
When I fall, the Valkyries will carry me to Valhalla. I've asked them to mind my bow.

~ opener realm
Jordheim's halls are mighty. I've been inside twice. Got lost once.
The jarls of Midgard feast all winter. I'm still waiting for my invite.
Yggdrasil holds up all nine worlds. I climbed it once. The small part.

~ opener fun
Why did Thor bring a ladder to the feast? The mead was on the high table. Ha!
Loki stole my boots once. Or I left them by the fire. It was Loki.

~ opener rvr
Albion's knights clank like a smithy in a storm. I hear them a mile off.
The Hibernians say their trees are magic. Our trees have wolves. Better trees.

~ opener life
Herring for breakfast, herring for lunch. Tonight, something new: smoked herring.

~ opener zones
{zone} would make a fine saga. Chapter one: wolves.

~ reply greeting
Hail, {0}! Sit by my fire, drink my mead, hear my saga. It is long.
Skol, {0}! May your axe be sharp and your beard well braided.
Well met, {0}, ring-giver! I have no rings. I just like the word.

~ reply farewell
Fare well, {0}. May the Norns spin you a long thread.
Go with Odin, {0}. Or with Thor. Thor is more fun.

~ reply banter
Ha! {0}, you'd make a fine skald. A bad one, but fine.
By Loki's grin, {0}, that's a good one.

~ reply abuse
Nithing! {0}, you speak like a troll with a toothache.
Mind your tongue, {0}, or I'll tell the skalds. They'll make a mean song of it.

~ reply grouping
A shield-wall! I'm in, {0}. Point me at the foe.
Aye, {0}! We'll go a-viking. On foot. My longship is in the shop.

~ reply help
Ask, {0}. The old runes know much, and I know some runes.

~ fallback
As the Allfather says, {0}: hm. He doesn't say much.
Aye, {0}. Ja. Truly.

~ ding
Level {level}! The skalds will sing of this! Quietly.
{level}! Odin sees all, and today he saw that.

~ grats
Skol, {0}! A horn of mead for you!
Well fought, {0}! Valhalla will keep you a seat. Not yet, though.

~ own_death
I fall! Valkyries, take me to Valhalla! ...They're late.
Dead. Not a glorious saga death. A small one.

~ other_death
Rise, {0}! Valhalla isn't ready for you.
{0} has fallen! I'll carve you a rune-stone. A small one.

~ drop
{item}! Plunder fit for a jarl!
{item}! Odin's ravens must have dropped it.

# Albion: Arthurian knightly English. Mixes up thy and thine on purpose (thine before a
# consonant, thy before a vowel, "is thy"), and a modern word slips into the odd grand speech.
=== fitzhunter
~ ambient
Forsooth, the road to Camelot is long, and my boots are most sore.
Hark! Doth any good knight seek a Scout for thine quest?
Verily, the arrow flieth true when the archer's heart is pure. And the ping is low.
Forsooth, Albion shall stand forever, m'lords and m'ladies!
Lay down thine weapons, foul beasts, for FitzHunter hath arrived!
Keep thine eyes on the treeline and thy hand on thy bow. Scout's rule, forsooth.
This longbow and I have seen many a battle. Most of them were, like, totally epic.

~ opener realm
In Camelot the king's feast was, forsooth, a total vibe.
Cotswold is the fairest village in all thy realm. Mine too. Ours, forsooth.
Hark, the walls of Camelot! I hath never seen walls so wall-like.

~ opener fun
Forsooth, a riddle: what hath four legs and smelleth of mead? My horse, after the tavern.
Prithee, who took mine arrows? Forsooth, 'twas me. I left them in a wolf.

~ opener rvr
Forsooth, the brutes of Midgard shall taste my arrows!
To the frontier, m'lords! For Albion, for honour, for thine loot!

~ opener life
The tavern's mead is most fine, forsooth. Ten out of ten, would quaff again.

~ opener zones
Forsooth, {zone} is a land most fair, and most full of things that bite.

~ reply greeting
Well met, {0}! Forsooth, thy coming gladdens my heart.
Hail and good morrow, {0}! How fareth thou upon thine journey?
M'lord {0}! Or m'lady. Forsooth, the helm makes it hard to tell.

~ reply farewell
Fare thee well, {0}. May thine aim be true and thy quiver full.
Godspeed, {0}. Forsooth, I shall miss thee. Hit me up later.

~ reply banter
Ha! Thou art a jester most fine, {0}. Forsooth, I nearly fell off my horse.
Thy wit is sharp, {0}, but mine is sharper. Forsooth.

~ reply abuse
Thou knave, {0}! I shall not break character for the likes of thee.
Forsooth, {0}, such words are most unknightly. Also kind of rude, dude.

~ reply grouping
Aye, {0}! My bow is thy. Lead on, forsooth.
A quest, {0}! Let us ride forth, as soon as I find my horse.

~ reply help
Fear not, {0}! FitzHunter shall aid thee, for thy cause is mine own.
Ask, {0}, and thou shalt receive. Unless it be gold. Forsooth, I have none.

~ fallback
Forsooth, {0}? Speak plainer, I prithee.
Verily, {0}. Verily indeed. Wait, what?

~ ding
Forsooth! Level {level}! My bow arm groweth stronger.
Hark! {level}! Thine faithful Scout riseth!

~ grats
Huzzah, {0}! Forsooth, thou art most mighty!
Well done, {0}! Thine glory is great.

~ own_death
Alas! I am slain! Forsooth, that was most uncool.
I perish! Tell the king... tell him the lag did it.

~ other_death
Alas, {0}! Thou hast fallen most nobly. Kind of.

~ drop
Forsooth! {item}! A treasure fit for thine king.
{item}! Verily, fortune smileth upon this humble Scout.

# Hibernia: the old tongue of the Celtic myths. Blessings, curses and the fair folk, with
# Gaelic-sounding words everywhere, some real (beannacht, slainte, a chara), some invented
# (glimmerach, rath-rinna, Brannaghmor). Kept in plain letters so every client shows them.
=== machunter
~ ambient
Beannacht na sidhe ort, whoever is listening. That's a blessing. Mostly.
The fair folk danced in the glen last night. I heard their pipes. Or a very drunk bard.
Slainte to the hills, slainte to the mists, slainte to whoever is buying.
My grandmother's grandmother saw a pooka. It stole her shoe and her good spoon.
May the rath-rinna guard this road. The rath-rinna is a spirit. I'm fairly sure.
Tir na nOg calls to me. It says: come home, and bring bread.
Go mbeannai Lugh do bhogha! Lugh bless your bow. I think that's what it means.

~ opener realm
Tir na nOg is the land of youth. I visited once. Came back with a cold.
The sidhe ride at Samhain. Lock your doors, and your pantry.
Every Hibernian knows the tale of Fionn and the salmon. I know three versions. All mine.

~ opener fun
Why don't the fair folk play cards? Too many tricks up their sleeves. Ah, sure.
A leprechaun once sold me a pot of gold. The pot was real.

~ opener rvr
Mallacht na bhfiach on the Albionach! The curse of the crows. May their arrows fly sideways.
To the frontier, a chairde! The banshee sings for our enemies, not us. I asked.

~ opener life
Stew for supper, blessed by the hearth-sprite Mairgreadoon. She likes extra salt.

~ opener zones
{zone} has a fairy ring. Don't step in it. Trust me.

~ reply greeting
Dia dhuit, {0}! May the sun of Lugh warm your back and the glimmerach keep your feet dry.
Failte, {0}, a chara! A blessing of the old hills on you, and a small one on your horse.
Well met, {0}! Beannacht na gaoithe ort, the blessing of the wind. May it never be in your face.

~ reply farewell
Slan abhaile, {0}! Safe home, and may the sidhe leave your boots alone.
Go n-eiri an bothar leat, {0}. May the road rise with you. Not too high, mind.
Off with you, {0}, under the blessing of Brannaghmor, keeper of lost socks.

~ reply banter
Ha! {0}, you've the tongue of a seanchai. That's a storyteller. A good one.
Och, {0}, the leprechauns would pay for that one. In fool's gold.

~ reply abuse
Mallacht ort, {0}! A curse on you. A mild one. Your bread will go stale.
May the pooka take your left boot, {0}. Only the left.

~ reply grouping
A fian! A war-band! I'm with you, {0}, by Nuada's silver hand.
Aye, {0}, I'll come. The druids say the omens are good. They always say that.

~ reply help
Help, {0}? Ask the hills. No, ask me. The hills never answer.
The old tales hold every answer, {0}. Pity I forgot most of them.

~ fallback
As the old ones say, {0}: dorcha an oiche, glas an bhrionnach. I'm not sure what it means either.
Och, {0}. Is that so.

~ ding
Level {level}! Lugh be praised, and the Dagda, and my old ma!
{level}! The sidhe sang for me. Or the wind. Both lovely.

~ grats
Comhghairdeas, {0}! Blessings of the nine hills on you!
Grats, {0}! May the fair folk fill your cup and leave your purse alone.

~ own_death
I'm slain! The banshee warned me. I thought she was just singing.
Dead. Mallacht! A curse on that beast's whole family tree.

~ other_death
Rest easy, {0}. The sidhe will mind you till you're back.
Och, {0}! A blessing on your poor bones.

~ drop
{item}! A gift from the fair folk. Or a trap. Taking it anyway.
{item}! Beannacht! The old gods are generous today.
